1
00:00:02,650 --> 00:00:05,310
I was eight the summer the Sun went out.

2
00:00:06,530 --> 00:00:08,320
I was drawing the Sun.

3
00:00:09,190 --> 00:00:11,525
Who draws at six in the morning?

4
00:00:11,895 --> 00:00:13,055
MIRA: Artists.

5
00:00:13,895 --> 00:00:16,280
And Biscuit was planning a crime.

6
00:00:16,650 --> 00:00:19,785
At that moment, half the
world was in daylight.

7
00:00:25,155 --> 00:00:26,545
Biscuit, no!

8
00:00:27,155 --> 00:00:28,890
Then the Sun went out.

9
00:00:33,160 --> 00:00:34,600
MIRA: Is it night again?

10
00:00:36,360 --> 00:00:38,870
It can't be night. It's breakfast time.

11
00:00:41,310 --> 00:00:44,020
The lights still worked. The Sun didn't.

12
00:00:44,390 --> 00:00:47,000
Then someone knocked. Three times.

13
00:00:47,990 --> 00:00:53,280
MR. ITO: Good morning. Your mother just phoned me
from the hospital. You're with me today.

14
00:00:53,650 --> 00:00:56,565
Shoes on, please. Both of you.

15
00:00:56,935 --> 00:01:02,445
Mr. Ito, from upstairs. He was
seventy-three, and he was never in a hurry.

16
00:01:02,815 --> 00:01:06,750
His bag was already packed. It
had been packed for years.

17
00:01:07,120 --> 00:01:10,285
MR. ITO: And bring the dog.
He has your toast.

18
00:01:10,655 --> 00:01:13,145
It took me a year to understand why.

19
00:01:14,315 --> 00:01:17,075
Outside, the whole street was looking up.

20
00:01:17,445 --> 00:01:20,680
Mum was a nurse. She was
stuck at the hospital.

21
00:01:21,050 --> 00:01:22,635
It's just an eclipse!

22
00:01:23,050 --> 00:01:27,915
MR. ITO: An eclipse never lasts longer
than seven and a half minutes.

23
00:01:28,285 --> 00:01:31,370
It was happening everywhere,
at the same moment.

24
00:01:32,040 --> 00:01:34,625
In New York, it was two in the morning.

25
00:01:35,295 --> 00:01:38,580
Most of them woke up to a
morning that never came.

26
00:01:39,350 --> 00:01:42,990
MR. ITO: Up to the roof. Kai,
bring my telescope.

27
00:01:43,360 --> 00:01:49,048
The lift was packed with neighbours, so Kai
carried it up six floors. He complained

28
00:01:49,048 --> 00:01:50,145
about every one.

29
00:01:50,515 --> 00:01:54,775
Mr. Ito came up here every
morning to watch the sunrise.

30
00:01:55,145 --> 00:01:57,680
That morning, he had seen the last one.

31
00:01:58,330 --> 00:02:00,720
MR. ITO: First, let's find Venus.

32
00:02:01,090 --> 00:02:03,250
MIRA: Why is that star still shining?

33
00:02:03,620 --> 00:02:09,766
MR. ITO: That's Venus. It shines with sunlight
bouncing off it, and that light takes a longer

34
00:02:09,766 --> 00:02:11,010
road to reach us.

35
00:02:11,380 --> 00:02:17,605
If something has happened to the Sun itself,
Venus will go dark too, at four minutes

36
00:02:17,605 --> 00:02:18,420
past seven.

37
00:02:18,790 --> 00:02:20,180
So we waited.

38
00:02:23,950 --> 00:02:27,090
MR. ITO: Then something has
happened at the Sun.

39
00:02:27,860 --> 00:02:33,750
Jupiter is much further away. Its light
should go out at four minutes past eight.

40
00:02:34,120 --> 00:02:36,585
At four minutes past eight, it did.

41
00:02:37,355 --> 00:02:42,570
He tapped the telescope three times. He
always did that. I never asked why.

42
00:02:51,640 --> 00:02:56,650
(RADIO) Stay indoors and keep warm. Power
and water are working normally.

43
00:02:57,020 --> 00:02:59,460
Everyone's going to the shops anyway.

44
00:03:00,480 --> 00:03:02,720
MR. ITO: Then so are we.

45
00:03:03,230 --> 00:03:06,695
Kai was right. The whole
city had the same idea.

46
00:03:07,065 --> 00:03:09,500
Rice. Tins. Warm socks. Seeds?

47
00:03:09,870 --> 00:03:11,635
MIRA: They're on Biscuit's list.

48
00:03:12,005 --> 00:03:15,515
Two grown men fought over the
last electric heater.

49
00:03:15,885 --> 00:03:21,200
MR. ITO: Heat won't be the hard part. Food
will. Nothing grows without the Sun.

50
00:03:21,570 --> 00:03:23,610
Kai! Mira! Can you see me?

51
00:03:24,330 --> 00:03:26,045
Mum! Are you okay?

52
00:03:26,415 --> 00:03:30,355
I'm fine, love. But I'm sleeping
here. They need every nurse.

53
00:03:30,725 --> 00:03:34,190
The ambulances haven't
stopped since it went dark.

54
00:03:34,560 --> 00:03:36,175
MIRA: When are you coming home?

55
00:03:36,560 --> 00:03:41,700
As soon as I can. I'll call every night
at seven. Be good for Mr. Ito.

56
00:03:42,070 --> 00:03:46,685
She called every night at seven.
Mr. Ito slept on our sofa.

57
00:03:47,055 --> 00:03:48,820
Is the Sun… dead?

58
00:03:49,490 --> 00:03:54,130
MR. ITO: If the Sun had vanished, its
pull on the sea would vanish too.

59
00:03:54,500 --> 00:03:58,490
And tomorrow's high tide
would be a third lower.

60
00:03:59,610 --> 00:04:04,700
Here's a tide table. Go and
check. High tide is at eleven.

61
00:04:05,070 --> 00:04:08,735
So the next morning, Kai and I
walked down to the harbour.

62
00:04:09,105 --> 00:04:10,770
MIRA: It's right on the line!

63
00:04:11,140 --> 00:04:13,850
So the Sun's still there. We
just can't see it.

64
00:04:14,220 --> 00:04:15,785
MIRA: Then why is it dark?

65
00:04:16,455 --> 00:04:20,315
The Sun hadn't gone anywhere.
Something was in front of it.

66
00:04:21,185 --> 00:04:23,250
MIRA: The fish haven't even noticed!

67
00:04:23,620 --> 00:04:27,210
NEWS ANCHOR: Infrared telescopes have
found something around the Sun.

68
00:04:27,580 --> 00:04:31,595
It's warm. It's round. And it's
twice as wide as the Sun.

69
00:04:32,265 --> 00:04:34,400
There was something around the Sun.

70
00:04:34,770 --> 00:04:37,085
A shell. Wrapped around a star.

71
00:04:38,255 --> 00:04:41,395
NEWS ANCHOR: Governments are
asking everyone to stay calm.

72
00:04:41,745 --> 00:04:43,385
Nobody was calm.

73
00:04:44,155 --> 00:04:46,265
MIRA: Mr. Ito, who built it?

74
00:04:47,785 --> 00:04:50,200
MR. ITO: It's bedtime, Mira.

75
00:04:50,570 --> 00:04:52,655
It was four in the afternoon.

76
00:04:53,305 --> 00:04:55,795
He'd never sent me to bed before.

77
00:04:56,565 --> 00:05:01,255
NEWS ANCHOR: A solar storm that left the Sun
before it went dark is reaching Earth tonight.

78
00:05:01,625 --> 00:05:03,290
MIRA: It's saying goodbye.

79
00:05:10,460 --> 00:05:13,300
By Day 7, it was twenty below.

80
00:05:13,670 --> 00:05:18,560
The pipes in our building froze and
burst, and the radiators went cold.

81
00:05:18,930 --> 00:05:23,090
All over the world, heating was
never built for cold like this.

82
00:05:23,460 --> 00:05:26,945
Mr. Ito showed us how to
keep one room warm.

83
00:05:27,315 --> 00:05:30,300
That night, Kai pretended
he wasn't scared.

84
00:05:30,670 --> 00:05:33,085
He was holding my hand too hard.

85
00:05:33,955 --> 00:05:35,695
Then we smelled smoke.

86
00:05:36,065 --> 00:05:41,005
MR. ITO: Their heating's off too, so
they're burning furniture. Indoors!

87
00:05:41,375 --> 00:05:47,678
Everyone out! A fire indoors makes carbon
monoxide. You can't see it or smell it,

88
00:05:47,678 --> 00:05:48,690
and it kills.

89
00:05:49,060 --> 00:05:51,645
Mr. Ito got the whole floor out.

90
00:05:52,015 --> 00:05:54,575
At the street door, Biscuit panicked.

91
00:05:54,945 --> 00:05:56,080
MIRA: BISCUIT!

92
00:05:57,450 --> 00:05:59,115
Biscuit! Come on, boy!

93
00:05:59,485 --> 00:06:04,923
The fire brigade put the fire out. Kai
searched all night, and came back without

94
00:06:04,923 --> 00:06:05,195
him.

95
00:06:05,565 --> 00:06:09,330
Mira, listen to me.
Biscuit is a clever dog.

96
00:06:09,700 --> 00:06:12,465
Clever dogs find warm places.

97
00:06:13,235 --> 00:06:15,445
MIRA: Kai, what's still warm out there?

98
00:06:15,795 --> 00:06:18,680
The metro! Warm air comes
up from the tunnels.

99
00:06:19,350 --> 00:06:21,310
Kai went straight back out.

100
00:06:21,950 --> 00:06:24,035
Paw prints. Fresh ones.

101
00:06:24,405 --> 00:06:27,390
They led to the only warm
thing on the street.

102
00:06:29,510 --> 00:06:31,675
You stupid, brilliant dog.

103
00:06:32,045 --> 00:06:37,280
A few metres down, the ground stays at
about ten degrees, Sun or no Sun.

104
00:06:37,650 --> 00:06:41,210
Biscuit hadn't run away. He'd
found the way down.

105
00:06:41,580 --> 00:06:45,165
By morning, half the street had
followed him underground.

106
00:06:45,535 --> 00:06:48,845
All over the world, cities
moved underground.

107
00:06:49,215 --> 00:06:53,000
By Day 30, Harbour Street
station was a town.

108
00:06:53,370 --> 00:06:57,755
The power stations kept running
on coal, gas and nuclear.

109
00:06:58,125 --> 00:07:01,060
Electric trains carried food and fuel.

110
00:07:01,430 --> 00:07:06,190
Anyone who could work from home did.
Everyone else worked underground.

111
00:07:06,560 --> 00:07:11,999
We grew lettuce, because it's ready in a
month. Potatoes. And mushrooms, which don't

112
00:07:11,999 --> 00:07:12,970
need any light.

113
00:07:13,340 --> 00:07:15,950
Everyone got very sick of lettuce.

114
00:07:16,320 --> 00:07:20,705
At seven every morning, the
engineers turned on the big lamps.

115
00:07:21,075 --> 00:07:23,215
MR. ITO: We'll call it sunrise.

116
00:07:24,335 --> 00:07:27,995
MIRA: It's yellow. And round. And
it's warm on your face.

117
00:07:28,365 --> 00:07:31,330
The littlest ones had
started drawing it grey.

118
00:07:32,300 --> 00:07:34,965
I kept mine in my pocket, all year.

119
00:07:35,735 --> 00:07:38,900
Biscuit got a fan club.
And a bigger belly.

120
00:07:39,270 --> 00:07:44,680
That month, the hospital opened a clinic in
our station. Mum asked to work there.

121
00:07:45,050 --> 00:07:46,815
And then she was home.

122
00:07:48,450 --> 00:07:50,340
You kept them alive.

123
00:07:50,710 --> 00:07:52,700
MR. ITO: They kept me alive.

124
00:07:59,670 --> 00:08:01,905
MIRA: Mr. Ito. Who built it?

125
00:08:02,275 --> 00:08:05,035
Nobody builds a thing that size in a day.

126
00:08:05,405 --> 00:08:07,970
MR. ITO: No. It's very old. Look.

127
00:08:09,005 --> 00:08:13,645
Nineteen-nineteen. A total
eclipse. Look at the lines.

128
00:08:14,015 --> 00:08:18,580
It was always there. Edge-on,
like the slats of a blind.

129
00:08:18,950 --> 00:08:23,090
On the morning the Sun went
out, every slat turned.

130
00:08:23,860 --> 00:08:25,975
MIRA: You've seen those lines before.

131
00:08:26,345 --> 00:08:30,085
MR. ITO: Once. A long time
ago. Nobody listened.

132
00:08:30,855 --> 00:08:34,815
I believed him. It was true. It
just wasn’t all of it.

133
00:08:35,785 --> 00:08:39,345
On Day 100, it was fifty below up top.

134
00:08:39,715 --> 00:08:43,925
The heaters that warm the air coming
down into the station froze.

135
00:08:44,295 --> 00:08:47,660
MR. ITO: I know a little
about old machines.

136
00:08:48,030 --> 00:08:49,990
That was the coldest night.

137
00:08:50,360 --> 00:08:53,170
All over the world, not everyone made it.

138
00:08:54,140 --> 00:08:56,205
How does this end, Mira?

139
00:08:57,375 --> 00:08:59,215
MIRA: With the Sun coming back.

140
00:09:00,185 --> 00:09:05,170
By morning, Mr. Ito and the engineers
had the heaters running again.

141
00:09:05,540 --> 00:09:07,000
MIRA: What are you building?

142
00:09:07,540 --> 00:09:10,730
MR. ITO: Something to listen
with. And to answer.

143
00:09:11,500 --> 00:09:16,385
NEWS ANCHOR: After months of searching, astronomers
have found where the Sun's light is going.

144
00:09:16,755 --> 00:09:22,495
One beam, aimed at a star
called HD 219134.

145
00:09:22,865 --> 00:09:26,930
And there's something inside it,
coming toward us and slowing down.

146
00:09:27,700 --> 00:09:30,340
MR. ITO: Twenty-one light-years.

147
00:09:31,710 --> 00:09:36,804
NEWS ANCHOR: At this rate, it will stop in
sixty-five days. Scientists hope the Sun will come

148
00:09:36,804 --> 00:09:37,950
back when it does.

149
00:09:38,720 --> 00:09:40,630
I didn't ask what it meant.

150
00:09:41,300 --> 00:09:45,885
All the Sun's light, pushing against
one small thing, coming our way.

151
00:09:51,255 --> 00:09:57,295
On Day 365, the escalators to the
surface had frozen solid.

152
00:09:57,665 --> 00:10:02,475
Kai carried Mr. Ito's dish all the way
up. He didn't complain once.

153
00:10:02,845 --> 00:10:08,347
Half the station came up with us. It was
seventy below, so we wore everything we

154
00:10:08,347 --> 00:10:08,760
owned.

155
00:10:09,130 --> 00:10:12,644
MIRA: One whole lap around the
Sun. Happy birthday, Earth.

156
00:10:13,014 --> 00:10:17,754
MR. ITO: It started at 6:52,
exactly one year ago.

157
00:10:18,124 --> 00:10:21,814
I think it stops at 6:52 today.

158
00:10:22,184 --> 00:10:25,474
Something near the Sun is calling us.

159
00:10:25,844 --> 00:10:26,629
One.

160
00:10:27,379 --> 00:10:27,914
Two.

161
00:10:28,664 --> 00:10:29,399
Three.

162
00:10:30,469 --> 00:10:32,159
Then it didn't stop.

163
00:10:33,029 --> 00:10:35,389
That's not three. That's hundreds.

164
00:10:35,759 --> 00:10:39,549
MR. ITO: One thousand, six
hundred and seventy-nine.

165
00:10:40,219 --> 00:10:41,754
Is that a lot?

166
00:10:42,324 --> 00:10:45,834
MR. ITO: It's twenty-three
times seventy-three.

167
00:10:46,504 --> 00:10:53,319
We used the same trick ourselves in
1974, to send a picture into space.

168
00:10:53,989 --> 00:10:55,979
MIRA: Like squares on graph paper.

169
00:10:56,629 --> 00:10:57,969
It's a picture!

170
00:10:58,839 --> 00:11:02,379
MR. ITO: Twenty-three across.
Seventy-three down.

171
00:11:02,839 --> 00:11:05,854
At the top: counting, from one to ten.

172
00:11:06,224 --> 00:11:09,459
Then them. And us, beside
them, very small.

173
00:11:10,229 --> 00:11:13,614
Their star. And their ship,
parked beside our Sun.

174
00:11:14,584 --> 00:11:16,719
MIRA: Mr. Ito? What is it?

175
00:11:17,089 --> 00:11:18,804
MR. ITO: It's a reply.

176
00:11:19,574 --> 00:11:20,589
MIRA: To who?

177
00:11:21,574 --> 00:11:22,864
MR. ITO: To me.

178
00:11:37,634 --> 00:11:42,449
The man in the observatory was Mr.
Ito, forty-four years ago.

179
00:11:43,219 --> 00:11:47,909
He found lines in old eclipse
photos. They pointed to one star.

180
00:11:48,279 --> 00:11:53,769
MR. ITO: It was a signpost. It meant: if
you can see this, call us. So I did.

181
00:11:54,139 --> 00:11:58,379
They told him not to. He knocked
anyway. One. Two. Three.

182
00:11:59,149 --> 00:12:04,789
MR. ITO: I sent one, two, three. They counted
on to ten, so I'd know it was them.

183
00:12:05,159 --> 00:12:10,332
His knock took twenty-one years to reach
them. They set off the day it arrived. It

184
00:12:10,332 --> 00:12:12,919
took them twenty-three years to get here.

185
00:12:13,289 --> 00:12:19,282
MR. ITO: Something that fast can't stop by
itself. It needs a push from the front. Only a

186
00:12:19,282 --> 00:12:21,379
whole star pushes that hard.

187
00:12:21,749 --> 00:12:28,073
They built that shell long ago, as a harbour,
so one day they could stop here. How,

188
00:12:28,073 --> 00:12:29,064
nobody knows.

189
00:12:29,434 --> 00:12:32,874
So for one year, our Sun was their brake.

190
00:12:33,244 --> 00:12:37,659
In a few minutes they'll have stopped,
and they won't need it anymore.

191
00:12:38,029 --> 00:12:42,844
I knew they might come any year now.
That's why my bag was packed.

192
00:12:43,614 --> 00:12:45,274
MIRA: Why didn't they warn us?

193
00:12:45,644 --> 00:12:49,159
MR. ITO: A warning can't
travel faster than light.

194
00:12:49,729 --> 00:12:53,894
They were almost that fast. It
would have arrived with them.

195
00:12:54,664 --> 00:12:56,499
MIRA: Did they know it would hurt us?

196
00:12:57,269 --> 00:12:58,784
MR. ITO: I don't know.

197
00:12:59,954 --> 00:13:02,119
I'm sorry, Mira.

198
00:13:03,489 --> 00:13:06,824
At the bottom of their picture
was an empty square.

199
00:13:07,594 --> 00:13:08,934
MIRA: It's our turn.

200
00:13:09,904 --> 00:13:13,639
Every radio telescope on Earth
got the same picture.

201
00:13:14,209 --> 00:13:17,819
Every government started arguing
about what to say back.

202
00:13:19,539 --> 00:13:21,049
MIRA: Can we send this?

203
00:13:21,419 --> 00:13:23,634
MR. ITO: Yes. I think we can.

204
00:13:24,404 --> 00:13:26,864
My drawing from the morning it went out.

205
00:13:27,214 --> 00:13:30,774
A big yellow sun. And me,
standing under it.

206
00:13:31,644 --> 00:13:33,929
Are we even allowed to do this?

207
00:13:34,279 --> 00:13:35,994
MR. ITO: Probably not.

208
00:13:36,964 --> 00:13:40,899
He pressed the key, the
way he had in 1983.

209
00:13:41,269 --> 00:13:42,959
Five minutes to go.

210
00:13:43,729 --> 00:13:47,139
The whole world was watching
the same patch of sky.

211
00:13:47,509 --> 00:13:48,924
MIRA: What if he's wrong?

212
00:13:49,274 --> 00:13:51,239
Then we'll wait together.

213
00:14:01,109 --> 00:14:02,369
MIRA: There it is!

214
00:14:03,539 --> 00:14:07,749
Everywhere it was daytime, the Sun
came back at the same moment.

215
00:14:14,319 --> 00:14:19,680
In New York it was the middle of the night.
They got their sunrise a few hours

216
00:14:19,680 --> 00:14:21,329
later, the ordinary way.

217
00:14:22,099 --> 00:14:27,409
At 6:52, exactly one year after it
went out, the Sun came back.

218
00:14:28,779 --> 00:14:33,664
Every morning for forty-three years,
Mr. Ito watched the sunrise.

219
00:14:34,634 --> 00:14:37,669
I used to think it was
because he liked it.

220
00:14:38,839 --> 00:14:40,929
He was waiting for an answer.

221
00:14:42,499 --> 00:14:46,734
Sixteen minutes after Mira pressed the
key, the dish clicked again.

222
00:14:49,899 --> 00:14:52,759
What would you have sent?
Tell us in the comments.
